# {{ display_name }} for iPhone, iPad and Mac

The Apple client of {{ display_name }}: its screens come from {{ origin }} and are drawn natively, by the Navree Native client{% if package_path %} in `{{ package_path }}`{% else %} ([`navree-swift`]({{ package_url }}) {{ package_version }}){% endif %}. Written by `navree native:init`.

## Run it

1. XcodeGen, once: `brew install xcodegen`.
2. In this folder: `xcodegen generate`, then `open {{ type_name }}.xcodeproj`.
3. Run the scheme `{{ type_name }}` in an iPhone or iPad simulator, or `{{ type_name }}Mac` on this Mac.

{% if loopback %}
The app talks to {{ origin }}: plain http to this machine, for development, so start the server there first (`navree dev`). A build for a device talks to the site over https: change `NavreeOrigin` in `project.yml`, for both apps.

{% endif %}
The server draws its pages for the app when its `navree` dependency has the native preview on: `navree native:doctor --platform ios` says what the server is missing, and `navree native:coverage --platform ios` how each page is drawn.

## Signing

`Signing/Signing.xcconfig` signs both apps with the bundle id `{{ bundle_id }}`{% if team %} and the team `{{ team }}`{% endif %}, and no entitlements. Sign in with Apple, passkeys, push and a sign-in that comes back on a link of the site need a team: copy `Signing/Signing.local.xcconfig.example` to `Signing/Signing.local.xcconfig`, fill it in (it says what to register with Apple and on the server), and run `xcodegen generate` again. The iPhone and iPad app and the Mac app share the bundle id, so one App ID and one `auth.apps.apple` entry on the server cover both.

## The client

{% if package_path %}
`project.yml` takes the client from `{{ package_path }}`: a Navree checkout's `packages/apple`, with its core built by `scripts/build-core.sh`. An app for people takes it from the package instead: `url: https://github.com/NavreeHQ/navree-swift` and `minorVersion:` the version of Navree the server runs.
{% else %}
`project.yml` takes the client from {{ package_url }}, up to the next minor version from {{ package_version }}. Keep it at the minor version of Navree the server runs: what a native client is answered may change in a minor release.
{% endif %}
Its runtime core is built for Apple silicon only: the Mac app is built for `arm64`, and a simulator build leaves out `x86_64`.

`{{ type_name }}/App.swift` is the whole app: a `ShellConfiguration` with the origin, and `NavreeScenes`. What else it can set (other hosts a web page loads from, covering the app in the app switcher, opening at login on a Mac) is in the client's documentation.
